WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.053
Dashboard design should begin with the decisions users need to make.

00:00:05.053 --> 00:00:16.171
Before choosing charts or software, identify who will use the system, what questions they must answer, how quickly they need answers, and what action should follow.

00:00:16.171 --> 00:00:22.437
A home user may need to distinguish routine motion from a meaningful perimeter event.

00:00:22.437 --> 00:00:28.299
A small business may monitor access, camera availability, endpoint alerts, and backup status.

00:00:28.299 --> 00:00:35.373
A laboratory or facility may care about restricted zones, environmental sensors, equipment health, and incident records.

00:00:35.373 --> 00:00:40.022
An enterprise team may need broader triage and ownership workflows.

00:00:40.022 --> 00:00:45.076
Situational awareness answers what is happening now and what changed recently.

00:00:45.076 --> 00:00:48.512
Alert triage helps determine priority and ownership.

00:00:48.512 --> 00:00:53.565
Event review connects an alert to supporting evidence and prior activity.

00:00:53.565 --> 00:00:58.214
Operational health shows whether the monitoring pipeline itself is functioning.

00:00:58.214 --> 00:01:01.246
Translate those goals into specific views.

00:01:01.246 --> 00:01:06.704
A triage view may emphasize new high-priority events, confidence, age, and assignment.

00:01:06.704 --> 00:01:12.161
An investigation view may show a timeline, related assets, logs, and clips.

00:01:12.161 --> 00:01:18.428
A health view may show failed collectors, storage pressure, delayed ingestion, and notification errors.

00:01:18.428 --> 00:01:22.672
Avoid a dashboard that displays data without supporting action.

00:01:22.672 --> 00:01:32.981
Large counters and colorful charts are not useful when a reviewer cannot tell what requires attention, why it matters, or where to investigate next.

00:01:32.981 --> 00:01:37.630
Every prominent element should support a defined decision or workflow.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.854
Set success measures early.

00:01:39.854 --> 00:01:49.758
Useful measures include time to acknowledge important alerts, time to find supporting evidence, false-positive burden, unresolved event age, ingestion delay, and service availability.

00:01:49.758 --> 00:01:55.620
These measures keep the dashboard focused on operational outcomes rather than visual complexity.
